          "eel becomes salmon-orange stripes or several separate toppings",
          "plate becomes a second rice mass",
          "food floats above plate"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "useful-form-and-plate-construction",
        "priority": 1,
        "visibleSuccess": "Preserve useful target design gains: a thick rounded eel arch with an understandable edge/underside, compact plump rice clusters supporting it, and a continuous smooth shallow ceramic dish with a deliberate rim/front thickness. Improve weak source construction when useful; exact old silhouette, decorative part count and tiny surface marks are not constraints.",
        "checkAt": [
          "enlarged crop",
          "native132px-wide asset",
          "66px standalone"
        ],
        "failureExamples": [
          "thin brittle eel ends",
          "melted or torn food contours",
          "unmotivated extra layers",
          "warped or broken elliptical rim",
          "rice occupies or obscures all plate surface"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "warm-soft-light-and-controlled-materials",
        "priority": 1,
        "visibleSuccess": "Broad warm light makes the caramel, ivory and olive forms readable through smooth midtones. Eel glaze has a few coherent honey/caramel ribbons and bounded reflections; rice feels softly rounded; nori has a restrained olive material response; the ceramic rim carries a gentle continuous reflection. The image feels warmer and softer than the original, while retaining legible depth. No preferred horizontal world light or directional ground cast shadow.",
        "checkAt": [
          "enlarged light/gray/dark composites",
          "66px standalone",
          "40px food"
        ],
        "failureExamples": [
          "dense white pinpoint glare dominates eel",
          "photographic mottled speckle competes with form",
          "cool patchwork plate shading dominates",
          "gloss becomes bloom or halo",
          "flat undifferentiated orange food"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "bounded-dark-regions-and-edible-detail",
        "priority": 1,
        "visibleSuccess": "The front/lower nori retains visible olive midtones and a coherent wrapped edge instead of a broad near-black dead area. Eel undersides, tail and contact seams may use deep brown locally when they explain thickness; they do not swallow the food shape. Rice reads as selected clustered grains at66px and40px, with detail subordinate to overall volume.",
        "checkAt": [
          "enlarged crop",
          "native asset",
          "66px standalone",
          "40px food"
        ],
        "failureExamples": [
          "lower nori is a featureless black rectangle",
          "dark eel lip merges with band into one mass",
          "sparkle or texture noise replaces rice structure",
          "every seam is brightened away so layers merge"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "actual-small-size-readability",
        "priority": 1,
        "visibleSuccess": "At66px standalone and40px bubble-food width, the caramel top, pale rice, dark olive band and complete pale plate remain distinct. At CELL32, use the full registered48x80px frame: dish is about16.5px wide and food inside the preference balloon10px wide. These tiny contexts retain a compact caramel/pale/dark-band recipe grouping and plate/balloon separation; individual grains need not survive. Show true-size views and enlarged inspections, clearly labeled.",
